/T (Virtual environments represent computer-generated spatial simulations that allow individuals to navigate and interact with simulated elements [1]. In their foundational work, Milgram and Kishino established the reality-virtuality continuum to classify systems spanning purely physical environments, augmented reality, and fully synthesized virtual worlds [1]. This theoretical model provides a framework for understanding how digital campus representations can range from 2D digital overlays to complete interactive digital twins [1]. Dionisio, Burns, and Gilbert examined the architectural evolution of 3D virtual worlds, documenting the technological prerequisites and interaction models needed to sustain engaging virtual spaces [2]. Their study emphasized that a practical virtual world requires more than static graphic rendering; it demands dynamic user agency, responsive object collision, and predictable event coordination between users and virtual assets [2].)
