/Title (The application architecture is organized into two primary workspaces: client and server [16]. The frontend client is developed with React 18, Vite, React Router, Tailwind CSS, and the Phaser 3 gaming framework [3], [4], [14], [15]. React governs top-level page views, navigation bars, search inputs, and modal interfaces, while Phaser manages the 2.5D canvas, sprite movements, collision boundaries, and camera tracking [3], [4]. This division of responsibility allows standard web interface elements to coexist with the game loop without degrading rendering performance [4], [13].)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2655 0 R
/Next 2673 0 R
>>
endobj
2673 0 obj
<<
/Title (The backend is engineered with Node.js and the Express framework to serve RESTful endpoints consumed by the client interface [16], [17]. Incoming network requests are routed to specialized controllers and services that manage location records, A* route calculations, assistant queries, user favourites, and virtual classroom sessions [17], [18]. By separating routing from underlying business logic, the server maintains clean modular boundaries that simplify long-term software maintenance and testing [18], [22].)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2655 0 R
/Prev 2672 0 R
>>
endobj
2674 0 obj
<<
/Title (across separate PDF circulars, static website directories, and generic web maps [7], [25]. When incoming students seek a specific lab or faculty room, they are forced to piece together information from disjointed sources [25]. Developing a unified browser-based virtual campus provides an intuitive bridge between visual layouts and facility details, allowing students to inspect rooms and plan walking routes within a single window [7], [25].)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2642 0 R
/Prev 2656 0 R
/Next 2657 0 R
>>
endobj
2675 0 obj
<<
/Title (of selected facilities across the IIIT Bhopal campus [2], [24]. The application integrates real- time avatar movement, landmark search, automated obstacle-avoiding route calculations, an intelligent campus guide, and interactive virtual classroom modules [2], [24].)
